---
/**
 * The dog, hidden in noise, between the last two GlizzyRepeats.
 *
 * A random-dot stereogram: relax your eyes past the screen and a hot dog turns
 * up in three dimensions. The engine is src/scripts/stereo-dog.js, shared with
 * the /stereo tuner so the two cannot drift.
 *
 * A titled `flush` WindowPanel. `flush` drops the bone card — that card exists
 * to put TYPE on, and this content is a full-bleed field of dots, so matting it
 * would read as a frame nothing chose.
 *
 * Bar and keyline are left alone — mustard, straight off windows.scss. That is
 * the crest.red trick the file describes: bar and border the same colour so the
 * two read as one drawn box rather than a strip sitting on a panel. Only the
 * label's alignment is overridden.
 *
 * The FIELD fills whatever space it is given — full width, flush with the other
 * windows — and the dog is sized conservatively inside it, so its edges are
 * always clear of the frame. Those two pull in opposite directions on a wide
 * screen and that is fine: `contain` binds the dog to the height, so it holds
 * one size while the backdrop grows around it. The extra backdrop is not waste
 * in a stereogram — it is the reference plane the eye locks onto.
 *
 * `flush` is kept for the one thing it does that is wanted here — dropping the
 * bone card, which exists to put TYPE on and would mat a field of dots with a
 * background nothing chose. Its other effect, zeroing the content padding, is
 * put back deliberately: that padding is what lets the panel's own dot screen
 * (the `.window::before` tile, mustard on a 3px grid over the backdrop blur)
 * show as a margin around the stereogram, and what keeps the square canvas away
 * from the 8px corner radius so the radius actually reads.
 *
 * The bar carries the NAME — "magic glizzy" — and the three views sit under
 * the image as buttons. It used to work the other way round: the bar said
 * "click to spin" and the whole panel was the control. That was one hidden
 * affordance doing one thing, and it could not grow a third state without the
 * label becoming a lie ("click to spin… or wiggle?"). Buttons say what is
 * available, say which one is on, and can be tabbed to.
 *
 * The SERVER-RENDERED markup states the still state — `aria-pressed="true"` on
 * `still`. The script would set it a moment later either way, but only once the
 * module has loaded and run: markup that ships claiming the opposite is wrong
 * for as long as the page is not yet interactive, which is exactly when someone
 * is reading it.
 *
 *   still   — default. Fusing takes several seconds of steady staring, and the
 *             image must not change while you do it, so this is the frame where
 *             people actually find the dog. It also costs nothing: the engine
 *             draws once and cancels its own loop.
 *   spin    — the dog turns. Capped to 24fps; nothing about a rotating dog
 *             needs 60, and the cap roughly halves the cost on a page already
 *             running the hero's dog and ScrollTrigger.
 *   wiggle  — the depth as motion parallax: the dot field sweeps sideways and
 *             back, the dog holds still. See makePlane()/makeSlide() in
 *             stereo-dog.js: every frame alone is blank noise, and the dog
 *             appears out of the boundary between what moves and what does
 *             not.
 *
 * WHY THE THIRD ONE EXISTS. A stereogram is invisible to anyone who cannot
 * free-view — through inexperience, or amblyopia, or one eye. Until now the
 * honest answer to "what am I supposed to be seeing" was "trust us". The
 * wiggle is the answer: same depth buffer, same dot field, same palette, laid
 * out in time rather than in space, and it asks nothing of the viewer. It is
 * also the fastest way for someone who DID fuse it to check they saw the dog
 * and not a dog-shaped wish.
 *
 * It is not the default, because it gives the answer away. Someone who lands on
 * `still` gets to find the dog themselves, which is the whole pleasure of the
 * thing; `wiggle` is there for when that has stopped being fun.
 */
import WindowPanel from './WindowPanel.astro';
---

<section class="glizzy-stereo">
  <WindowPanel flush title="magic glizzy">
    <div
      class="glizzy-stereo__field"
      id="glizzy-stereo-field"
      role="img"
      aria-label="A hidden-image stereogram of the dog. Relax your eyes past the screen and a hot dog appears in three dimensions. Currently still."
    >
      <canvas class="glizzy-stereo__canvas" id="glizzy-stereo-canvas"></canvas>
    </div>
    <div class="glizzy-stereo__views" role="group" aria-label="How to view the stereogram">
      <button type="button" class="glizzy-stereo__view" data-view="still" aria-pressed="true">still</button>
      <button type="button" class="glizzy-stereo__view" data-view="spin" aria-pressed="false">spin</button>
      <button type="button" class="glizzy-stereo__view" data-view="wiggle" aria-pressed="false">wiggle</button>
    </div>
  </WindowPanel>
</section>

<style lang="scss">
  /*
   * No inline padding. `main` already sets the one page gutter — see the long
   * note on --gutter in vars.scss about sections that each added their own on
   * top and ended up 16px out of line with each other. This panel was doing
   * exactly that: every other window sits flush at the gutter and this one was
   * inset a further 16px.
   */
  /*
   * The panel fills its SECTION, rather than being a fixed slab sitting inside
   * one. base.scss gives every `section` a min-height of nearly a viewport, so
   * a `clamp(320px, 62vh, 680px)` panel left roughly a third of the section as
   * dead navy underneath it — visible the moment the section box is inspected.
   *
   * Flex rather than `height: 100%`, so it stretches to whatever the section
   * actually resolves to (the min-height, or taller if something else pushes
   * it) without needing to restate the rule and drift from it.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo {
    width: 100%;
    display: flex;
    /*
     * The panel fills the section, so the FIELD's height is the section's less
     * the section's padding and the panel's own furniture. base.scss gives
     * every section `100svh - 120px`, which after its 48px top and bottom left
     * the panel at `100svh - 216px` — and the view row then took another ~51px
     * out of the image from the inside. One viewport here puts the panel at
     * `100svh - 96px` and hands that back with room over.
     *
     * This is also how the panel is kept under 100svh: BY CONSTRUCTION, not by
     * a cap. A `max-height` on `.window` is the one thing windows.scss tells
     * you not to add back (see its "NO viewport cap" note) — with the panel's
     * `overflow: hidden` and no inner scroll, a cap does not clip gracefully,
     * it just hides whatever did not fit. The section is a border-box, so its
     * own padding guarantees 96px of headroom and the panel cannot reach the
     * viewport height. The one case a cap WOULD bite — a landscape phone,
     * where the field's 320px minimum plus the bar and the row exceed the
     * screen — is exactly the case where clipping would eat the view buttons,
     * and growing past the fold is the better failure.
     */
    min-height: 100svh;
  }
  .glizzy-stereo :global(.window) { flex: 1 1 auto; }

  /*
   * Trim the section's vertical padding on a phone. base.scss gives every
   * section 48px top and bottom, which is right for a section of type and
   * expensive here: it comes straight off the panel's height, and height is
   * what a stereogram has least of on a phone. 16px buys back 64px of field
   * without touching the rhythm between sections on wider screens, where the
   * space is not scarce.
   */
  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .glizzy-stereo { padding-block: 16px; }
  }


  /*
   * Only the COLOUR is set, on both bar and keyline together, so the 2px weight
   * and the bar's metrics keep coming from windows.scss and cannot drift from
   * the other panels.
   *
   * `:global` because `.window` belongs to WindowPanel and never carries this
   * component's scope attribute; the descendant selector still scopes it to
   * this section, and at (0,2,0) it clears the (0,1,0) defaults outright rather
   * than tying with them — a tiebreak settled by file order has gone wrong in
   * this repo before.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo :global(.window-chrome) {
    justify-content: center;
  }

  /*
   * Puts back the padding `flush` removed, so the panel's dot screen frames the
   * image instead of the canvas running edge to edge under the keyline. `flush`
   * is still doing its real job here: no bone card.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo :global(.window--flush .window-content) {
    padding: 1rem;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 0;
  }

  /*
   * Still a plain div, even though it now takes a click. The three buttons
   * below carry the KEYBOARD interaction and the pressed state; making this a
   * button as well would put a second control in the tab order that reaches a
   * subset of the same views and announces itself worse. So the click here is
   * a pointer-only shortcut to a control that already exists — the rule being
   * that nothing is reachable ONLY by clicking the image.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo__field {
    display: block;
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    /* grows into whatever the panel has left under the chrome bar and the
       view row — both of those are fixed-height, this is the one that flexes */
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 320px;
    background: #0a0a0a;
    /*
     * 4px, not the panel's 8. `.window-content__inner` is the precedent for an
     * inner radius in this system, and a box sitting inside 1rem of padding can
     * never be concentric with its parent's corner anyway — the concentric
     * inner radius for an 8px corner inset by 16px is 0. Matching the 8 would
     * read as a second frame competing with the keyline rather than as the
     * image's own edge.
     *
     * The stroke is `--bg-bottom`, at the 2px keyline weight every window on
     * the site is drawn with. It is the darker of the page's two grounds, so
     * against the panel's dot screen it reads as a definite edge while staying
     * inside the palette — black would be the only true black anywhere on the
     * page. It sits one step under the stereogram's own navy ground
     * (`--bg-top`), which is what makes the image end somewhere rather than
     * dissolving into the mustard.
     *
     * This is the one place `--bg-bottom` earns its keep near the stereogram.
     * It is deliberately kept OUT of the dot palette — 24 apart from
     * `--bg-top` in blue alone is the same colour twice in a dot field, and
     * raises the coincidental-match rate — but as a line against a boundary
     * that is exactly the relationship wanted.
     *
     * `overflow: hidden` was already here, so the canvas is clipped to the new
     * radius with nothing further to do. It is measured with
     * getBoundingClientRect on this element, which under `box-sizing:
     * border-box` includes the 2px — so the dot grid runs 4px wider and taller
     * than the visible area and loses an edge cell under the stroke. That is
     * the same clipping the canvas was always designed around, and the repeat
     * period, which is the only number fusing depends on, is untouched by it.
     */
    border: 2px solid var(--bg-bottom);
    border-radius: 4px;
    overflow: hidden;
    /* The field is a click target now — see the toggle below. `manipulation`
       kills the double-tap zoom delay without taking pan or pinch away, which
       `none` would: that mistake cost this page 65% of a phone screen once. */
    cursor: pointer;
    touch-action: manipulation;
  }

  /*
   * The view row. Monospace, uppercase, 0.18em — the same face and tracking as
   * `.window-title` in windows.scss, because these are the panel's own
   * furniture and should read as part of the frame rather than as page CTAs
   * that wandered inside it. 2px borders for the same reason: that is the
   * keyline weight every window is drawn with.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo__views {
    display: flex;
    gap: 6px;
    padding-top: 1rem;
    flex: 0 0 auto;
  }
  /*
   * `flex: 1 1 0` — a ZERO basis, so the three share the row equally rather
   * than in proportion to their labels. `1 1 auto` would make WIGGLE the widest
   * and STILL the narrowest, which reads as a ranking of three things that are
   * peers. `min-width: 0` because a flex item's default `min-width: auto`
   * refuses to shrink below its content and would put the row back out of
   * balance on a narrow phone.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo__view {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
    font: 11px/1 ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, monospace;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    padding: 8px 10px;
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--mustard);
    /*
     * Opaque, not transparent. The panel's dot screen runs behind this row, and
     * a transparent button put that texture directly under 11px mono set at
     * 0.18em — the one thing the whole design is otherwise careful never to do.
     * Navy is also what the pressed state uses as its INK, so the two states
     * are one pair of colours swapping places rather than three colours.
     */
    background: var(--bg-top);
    border: 2px solid var(--mustard);
    border-radius: 2px;
    cursor: pointer;
    touch-action: manipulation;
  }
  /*
   * The on state is the FILL, not a colour shift. A stereogram sits next to
   * this and the eye is about to be asked to hold a difficult convergence on
   * it; two states told apart by a shade of mustard is exactly the kind of
   * low-contrast judgement to keep away from that.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo__view[aria-pressed='true'] {
    background: var(--mustard);
    color: var(--bg-top);
  }
  .glizzy-stereo__view:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--bone); outline-offset: 2px; }

  /*
   * Centred and clipped, never scaled. The engine renders a grid at least as
   * big as this box (`cover`), so the sub-dot surplus is trimmed evenly on both
   * axes and no background shows at the edges.
   */
  .glizzy-stereo__canvas {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    image-rendering: pixelated;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { createStereoDog, presetForViewport } from '../scripts/stereo-dog.js';

  const canvas = document.getElementById('glizzy-stereo-canvas');
  const field = document.getElementById('glizzy-stereo-field');
  const section = document.querySelector('.glizzy-stereo');
  const viewBtns = section ? [...section.querySelectorAll('[data-view]')] : [];

  if (canvas && field) {
    const still = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
    const SPIN = 100;

    /* settings follow the device, not the container — see presetForViewport */

    const dog = createStereoDog(canvas, {
      /*
       * spin: 0 — STILL until someone asks for motion, for everyone, not just
       * for prefers-reduced-motion. See the note at the top: fusing takes
       * several seconds of steady staring and the image changes while it turns,
       * so the still frame is the one where people actually find the dog, and
       * it is also the frame that costs nothing.
       *
       * presetForViewport() is applied just below and deliberately carries no
       * `spin`, so it cannot put this back. If a spin key is ever added there,
       * this stops working and the only symptom is a dog that spins on load.
       */
      params: {
        spin: 0,
        /*
         * Two-tone for every view — navy, one bone dot in four. This was a
         * wiggle-only aid until it turned out to be the setting that made the
         * wiggle readable, and the stereogram then had to wear the same field:
         * three views of one thing should not look like three things. The
         * four-colour palette below still decides WHICH two — darkest and
         * lightest — and is what the colour sheet compared. See paint() in
         * stereo-dog.js for what it costs the stereogram.
         */
        tone: 1,
      },
      maxFps: 24,
      /*
       * Palette 4.15 off /stereo-colours: mustard, bone, cta, bg-top. Chosen by
       * eye from the sheet, which is the only way that judgement gets made.
       *
       * It drops both oranges, so the four sit at four clearly separated
       * luminances — near-black navy, mid blue, bright mustard, near-white bone
       * — and nothing collapses into its neighbour the way the all-warm sets do.
       */
      palette: ['#ffa300', '#f7e0c5', '#30a2ff', '#000d60'],
      cover: true,
      pauseWhenOffscreen: true,
      /* Full-bleed is a lot of cells. The engine coarsens the dot rather than
         dropping frames, and holds the on-screen repeat at 101px whatever the
         dot size, so fusing is unaffected. */
      maxCells: 220000,
      measure: () => field.getBoundingClientRect(),
    });
    dog.start();

    /*
     * A handle on the running engine, so /stereo can render THIS component and
     * drive it — tuning the real thing in its real frame rather than a bespoke
     * layout that has to be kept in sync by hand. Same shape as hero-dog.js's
     * `window.glizzy3D`. The event covers the case where the tuner's script
     * runs first; the global covers the case where it runs second.
     */
    const applyPreset = () => {
      const preset = presetForViewport(window.innerWidth);
      for (const k in preset) dog.setParam(k, preset[k]);
    };
    applyPreset();

    let periodRid = null;
    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      clearTimeout(periodRid);
      periodRid = setTimeout(applyPreset, 200);
    });

    window.glizzyStereo = dog;
    document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('stereo:ready', { detail: dog }));

    /*
     * One switch for all three views, rather than a spin toggle with a wiggle
     * bolted beside it. They are mutually exclusive — the wiggle's two slides
     * are built from a still pose, and spinning while it flips would be two
     * kinds of motion arguing — so a single function that puts the engine in
     * exactly one state cannot leave it in a combination nobody designed.
     */
    const VIEW_LABEL = {
      still:
        'A hidden-image stereogram of the dog. Relax your eyes past the screen and a hot dog appears in three dimensions. Currently still.',
      spin: 'A hidden-image stereogram of the dog, currently spinning. The still view is easier to see.',
      wiggle:
        'The dot field sweeping from side to side while the hidden dog holds still, which makes it appear as a shape. No relaxed focus needed.',
    };

    let view = 'still';

    function setView(next) {
      view = next;
      /* Order does not matter to the engine — both call invalidate() — but the
         pair is built from the pose, so spin goes off in the same breath. */
      dog.setView(next === 'wiggle' ? 'wiggle' : 'sirds');
      dog.setSpin(next === 'spin' ? SPIN : 0);
      for (const b of viewBtns) b.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(b.dataset.view === next));
      field.setAttribute('aria-label', VIEW_LABEL[next] || VIEW_LABEL.still);
    }

    for (const b of viewBtns) b.addEventListener('click', () => setView(b.dataset.view));

    /*
     * Clicking the image itself toggles spin. It is the gesture people try
     * first — this panel WAS a single tap-to-spin button before the views
     * existed — and losing it entirely to a button row was a step backwards for
     * anyone using a phone.
     *
     * It runs through setView(), not dog.setSpin(), so the buttons and the
     * accessible name cannot fall out of step with the engine. Which means it
     * also resolves the third state honestly: a click from WIGGLE goes to spin
     * rather than doing nothing, because "not spinning" is the branch it
     * matches, and the alternative — a click that is silently inert in one of
     * three views — is the kind of dead affordance this row was built to
     * remove.
     */
    field.addEventListener('click', () => setView(view === 'spin' ? 'still' : 'spin'));

    /*
     * `still.matches` is NOT consulted when a button is pressed, and that is
     * deliberate. prefers-reduced-motion governs motion the user did not ask
     * for, and nothing here starts moving on its own: the default view is
     * still, for everyone. Gating the buttons on it would make them silently
     * inert for those users while sitting there offering `spin` and `wiggle` —
     * a control that lies is worse than the motion. What the setting does do is
     * stop motion already running if it is switched on mid-visit.
     */
    still.addEventListener('change', (e) => {
      if (e.matches && view !== 'still') setView('still');
    });

    setView('still');
  }
</script>
